Progress status for this period: regressing. This was computed from strengthIndex by a fixed rule, so treat it as settled and use progressiveOverload.notes to explain it with the specific numbers. The rule: regressing if any muscle group's index dropped 3+ points, otherwise on_track if any rose 3+ points, otherwise stalling; changes are measured against previousIndex, or against 100 (the user's first recorded level) when previousIndex is null.

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. Only make physiological claims that are well established — for example, do not claim that training one muscle group raises hormone levels enough to drive muscle growth elsewhere. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Strength dipped across your main lifts this week — time to prioritize recovery and reset.",
  "summary": "This week showed a regression across your major muscle groups, with chest, back, and legs all seeing meaningful drops in their strength index. The good news is that your overall training volume remains solid at 28 sets and over 10,700 kg of total tonnage, which shows you are still putting in real work. At 28 years old with a healthy BMI of 23.5, your body is well-positioned to recover and bounce back strong. The dip in strength numbers is a signal worth taking seriously, but it is not a cause for alarm — it is your body telling you it needs a little more support to keep growing.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "notes": "This period is classified as regressing, and the numbers back that up clearly. The chest strength index fell from 106 to 101, a drop of 5 points. The back fell from 104 to 100, a drop of 4 points. Legs dropped from 108 to 102, a drop of 6 points — the largest decline this period. Shoulders held steady at 103, biceps held at 102, and triceps held at 104, which is encouraging and shows the smaller muscle groups are maintaining their baseline. However, because all three primary movers — chest, back, and legs — each dropped 3 or more points, the overall verdict is regression. This pattern, where the big compound-driven groups all decline together in the same period, typically points to accumulated fatigue, insufficient recovery between sessions, or a caloric or sleep deficit rather than a problem with any single exercise.",
    "status": "regressing"
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps volume sits at just 4% of total training volume with only 3 sets, compared to 6 sets each for chest and back. For a muscle-building goal, this is on the low end and may limit arm development relative to the larger pulling muscles over time."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Shoulders",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Shoulders account for only 10.4% of volume with 4 sets. Given that chest and back each receive 6 sets, the shoulders are comparatively under-served and may lag in development, particularly in lateral and rear delt growth which support both aesthetics and joint health."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": true,
  "deloadReason": "All three primary muscle groups — chest, back, and legs — each experienced a strength index decline of 4 to 6 points in a single period. This simultaneous regression across the main compound movements is a strong indicator of systemic fatigue. A deload, reducing volume and intensity for one period, will allow your nervous system and muscles to recover fully so that you can return to progressive overload rather than continuing to dig a deeper fatigue hole.",
  "actionItems": [
    "During your deload, reduce working sets by roughly 40 to 50 percent across all muscle groups and drop loads to a comfortable, controlled level — this is active recovery, not a week off, so keep moving.",
    "Audit your sleep and nutrition this week: consistent regressions across all major lifts often trace back to poor sleep quality or a caloric deficit, so aim for at least 7 to 9 hours of sleep and ensure you are eating enough protein and total calories to support muscle building at your bodyweight.",
    "After the deload, bring biceps and shoulder volume up slightly — consider adding one set each to bring them more in line with the volume given to chest and back, supporting more balanced long-term muscle development.",
    "When you return to full training, reintroduce progressive overload gradually rather than jumping straight back to previous loads — aim to match your prior strength index numbers first, then push for new personal bests."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Regression period with strength index drops of 5 points in chest, 4 in back, and 6 in legs; a deload was recommended to address systemic fatigue before resuming progressive overload."
}
